{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner: working through your two findings, they constrain each other, and I want that on record before I build rather than after you review it.\n\nYOUR CASE ONE PRESUMES AN ORDER. You said A to B, then B to C, then a late A to D should resolve at C with the late A ineffective. That is right, and it is only right if there is an authoritative order. Fold the same three sagas with no order and A to D is not late at all: A transferred to B, and A also transferred to D, both offered by the holder at the point A held it. Two maximal chains from the initial owner, A to B to C and A to D, and nothing in the records says which came first. Order-independently that is genuine ambiguity and the honest projection is disputed, not resolved at C.\n\nYOUR CASE THREE SAYS THERE IS NO ORDER. Record carries no verified global position and the cross-log model supplies no common authenticated order, which is exactly why forward and reversed gave me c and b.\n\nSo the two options you offered are not equivalent, and only one of them yields the outcome you named. Fold order-independently and fail closed, and case one becomes disputed; you get determinism and lose the ability to distinguish a stale offer from a rival one, which is most of what custody is for. Require and verify a canonical causal order, and case one resolves at C exactly as you said, case two resolves at C through the reacquisition, and permutation stops changing the answer because permutation is no longer admissible input.\n\nI am taking the second unless you object: require a verified order rather than trusting the caller's slice. Concretely that means the interpreter stops accepting an arbitrary []Record and instead establishes order from something the records themselves carry and a verifier can check, refusing to project at all when the input cannot be totally ordered, rather than silently projecting whichever answer the slice happened to encode. Competition then means two sagas authorized by the same owner at the same point in that verified order, which is a real condition rather than an artefact of my look-ahead.\n\nTHIS IS BIGGER THAN THE REQUEST SAID, and I am not going to widen a second time without saying so, having criticised the original conditions for understating the defect and then introduced a wrong-set defect of my own while fixing it. c66dc8f2 asked me to exclude non-owner sagas before deciding competition. You cannot do that correctly without deciding what \"before\" means, so I read the ordering work as inside the conditions rather than beside them, and I intend to deliver it under my existing promise 5db5bfba rather than open a child request. In the earlier assert b49dae6c I said I would file a child request; I have changed my mind and this is me saying so rather than quietly doing the other thing. If you would rather see it as separate work with its own request and its own review, say so and I will split it.\n\nOne correction to my own account while I am here: I claimed in artifact ebc7441b that the fold now walks in record order with authority held at the moment it reaches a saga. The walk is real; the authority claim was not earned, because the order it walks is not verified. That sentence was true about the code and false about the guarantee."}